This gives you a single source of truth (one GitHub repository) while each platform automatically deploys only the part of the codebase it is responsible for.

One architectural refinement I'd suggest

Since your platform is evolving into a full enterprise security product, I'd avoid putting your primary backend API on Vercel. Instead:

Vercel → Next.js frontend (and only lightweight serverless endpoints if truly needed)
Cloudflare → DNS, CDN, WAF, SSL, caching, DDoS protection
Supabase → Authentication, PostgreSQL, Storage, Realtime
DigitalOcean Kubernetes → Backend API, scanner orchestration, all scanner engines, report generation, background workers, AI services, and Redis

That separation keeps the frontend fast, the backend fully under your control, and makes it much easier to scale scanners independently from the user interface. As QGuard Helix grows, this architecture is one that many production SaaS platforms follow because each component has a clear responsibility and can evolve without affecting the others.